 { In this section we'll cover three related topics: 1. Damaging pawns by splitting, doubling or isolating them. 2. Improving your own weak pawns 3. The Minority Attack, a positional strategy designed to weaken the opponent's pawns. We'll begin this section by showing an example of one of the most frequent methods used to undermine an opponent's structure: splitting the pawn chain. In this online game, Magnus Carlsen has a promising position straight out of the opening and could continue in several ways. } 1. { -KEY- } e6 $1 { He chooses the most aggressive path, gambiting a pawn in order to fracture Black's kingside pawn chain. } fxe6 { Black has little choice but to accept the gambit. Now their kingside pawn chain loses its integrity and the g6 pawn becomes an easy target. } ( 1... Nb6 { In the game Black ignored the pawn, but the position is still close to hopeless. } 2. exf7 Kxf7 { Not only are the king and the g6 pawn weak, but White has also picked up outposts on e5 and g5. } 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Ne5 Kg8 5. Nxg6 $18 { White is now up a pawn on top of his other positional advantages. } ) 
2. Bd3 Nf8 3. Nf3 $18 { Quiet development is very typical after such pawn sacrifices. The purpose of White's play is not to rush to recapture the pawn, but rather to sow discord among Black's forces and exploit the opponent's enduring lack of piece coordination over the coming handful of moves. Since Black has no king safety or ways to develop his pieces, the position is already lost. } 
