 { Can you find the strongest move for White here, with the aim of damaging the opponent's structure? } 1. { -KEY- } Bd6 $1 { A very clever move! White targets the rook on f8, forcing Black to spend time dealing with the threat. } Re8 2. g4 $1 { Without their bishop sitting on g3, White is now able to push the g-pawn forward. } Bg6 3. Bxg6 $1 { Correct! } fxg6 { The result of the sequence is that now Black has been left with a backward pawn on e6 and doubled pawns on the g-file. The change in structure has also left Black's king more vulnerable to attack in the long term. } 4. Bg3 $16 { White returns their bishop home, and over the coming moves will look to double up rooks along the e-file to pressure Black's now backward pawn on e6. Black's lack of counterplay leaves him with a near-hopeless position. In this position we see that Black's pawn on e6 is effectively isolated as it cannot receive support from any pawn. Let's check out another example of this in action. } 
