 { White may have doubled c-pawns, but overall their pawn centre looks pretty strong. Black's next move aims to break this harmony. } 1... { -KEY- } e5 $1 { Gambiting a pawn, which White is compelled to accept. } 2. dxe5 { White has nothing better than to accept the sacrifice, but once again it leads to a split pawn chain. The doubled and isolated c-pawns are so weak that Black has no concern being a pawn down for now. } ( 2. Bg3 $2 { White's position can quickly collapse if they try to keep the pawn tension. } Qe7 3. Be2 f4 $1 { A second pawn break. } 4. exf4 exd4 $1 { An eye for an eye in terms of pawns, but now White's structure is completely crippled and it's also hard to avoid losing further material. For example: } 5. cxd4 Nxd4 6. Rg1 O-O-O { -+ Black is completely winning. } ) 
 ( 2. Bxh6 $6 { This move is playable, but very risky. For instance, after } Qf6 3. Bg5 Qh8 { it turns out that White has no great way of dealing with the threat of ...Rxg5. I suspect most humans here would play } 4. Rg1 { but after } Rxg5 $1 { they would have to resign, since } 5. hxg5 Qh2 $19 { wins the White rook. } ) 
Qe7 $1 3. Rb5 { White tries to keep the extra pawn. } O-O-O $1 { Black is in no rush to recover the pawn, first bringing the king to safety. } ( 3... Bc5 $6 { This move does not work, since Black has no convincing reply against Nb3. } ) 
4. Be2 d6 $1 { Not worrying about the material count, since being down a pawn is not a big concern when White is underdeveloped and has a fractured structure on the queenside. } 5. exd6 Bxd6 6. Rxf5 { White grabs a second pawn, not so much out of material greed but in order to defend their bishop on f4. } ( 6. O-O $2 { If White tries to rush the king to safety, Black will quickly convert their positional plusses into a crushing attack. } Bxf4 7. exf4 Rge8 8. Re1 g3 $1 { The killer blow. Black is fighting for access to the e3 square. } 9. Rd5 ( 9. fxg3 $2 { loses a piece after } Qe3 ) 
gxf2 10. Kxf2 Qxh4 11. Kf1 Ne7 12. Rxd8 Rxd8 $19 { White cannot stop all of Black's threats. } ) 
Bxf4 7. Rxf4 Ne5 { White's poor structure makes their two-pawn advantage irrelevant, while Black's threats across the board are impossible to parry. The position remains messy but objectively Black is winning. Here is just one possible variation: } 8. Rd4 Rdf8 9. e4 { Trying to shut out Black's strong bishop. } Qf6 10. Rf1 Qxh4 $19 { White's king remains under constant fire, while Black can aim to pry open new lines with ...g3 in the future. Since a position can degrade very quickly when your structure gets targeted, it's important to seek out ways to avoid this from happening to you whenever possible. Let's take a look at one more example on this theme, but this time around Black turns the tables on White. } 
