 { White has just played the move g2-g4, going hunting for the bishop on f5. But Black has a clever defense. Can you find the saving sequence? } 1... { -KEY- } Nxe5 $1 ( { The problem with } 1... Bg6 $2 { is that after } 2. h4 $1 { White is now threatening to trap the bishop with h4-h5, and if Black replies with a move like } h6 { , then after } 3. Nxg6 $16 { Black's structure gets split, in a similar way to the first couple of examples of the section. } ) 
2. dxe5 { It seems like Black is in trouble since they have two minor pieces under attack... } Nxg4 $1 { Black's tactical shot turns the tables on White. } 3. fxg4 $2 { White wrongly accepts the sacrifice. } ( 3. Bf4 { was the only path to maintain rough equality, with the point being that after } Nh6 4. Qd2 $13 { an unclear position has arisen, with Black's extra pawn largely offset by White's development lead and attacking prospects on the kingside. } ) 
Qh4+ { Correct! Black succeeds in punishing White's risky pawn advance on the kingside. } 4. Kd2 Be4 $1 5. Rg1 ( 5. Nxe4 $2 dxe4 { Black will soon recover their piece, with a crushing attack to boot. } 6. Kc3 Rd8 7. Bd2 e3 $19 ) 
Qxh2 $19 { There is no good way of defending the rook, and so Black is winning. In general, you should seek to avoid damage to your structure by positional means when possible. However, if these are not available it is a good idea to scout for a tactical solution. One of the most common ways in which I have seen intermediate-level students lose a game is by letting the opponent accumulate positional pluses too easily. In practice, it will often be better to play down material in a complicated position than to accept a damaged structure in a simple one. You can also use tactical alertness to improve your own structure, as will be seen in the next couple of examples. } 
