 { This example shows the same idea but with less high stakes. Here, White again should take the opportunity to 'correct' their structure. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxa6 $1 { White initiates the exchange, forcing Black to misplace their knight upon recapturing. } ( { The game itself continued as follows: } 1. Re1 $6 Bxd3 2. Qxd3 Nd7 3. e4 $6 { Pushing the wrong pawn. White still had reason to hope for a slight edge had they chosen to play c3-c4 instead. } dxe4 4. Rxe4 Qd6 $11 { White has a bit more space in the centre, but that is easily offset by Black's more compact pawn structure. Moreover, from a practical point of view I think it's easier for White to get into trouble due to the vulnerability of their a- and c-pawns. } ) 
Nxa6 2. Qa4 $1 { Gaining time against the undefended knight, and also eyeing the c4 square. } Nb8 3. c4 $1 dxc4 4. Qxc4 $14 { White has the better chances here, having got rid of their weakest pawn and at the same time strengthened their advantage in the centre. To make matters worse for Black, they do not have a straightforward way of developing their knight due to the pressure against c7. For the last examples of the section, we'll cover a well-known strategy designed to damage the opponent's structure: the 'Minority Attack'. } 
