 { The 'Minority Attack' is a popular pawn break strategy that is frequently possible in the so-called 'Carlsbad Structure'. This structure arises mostly from queen's pawn openings and involves one side possessing a 5 & 2 pawn chain while the other side has a 4 & 3 pawn chain, as in the position below. The purpose of a minority attack is to favorably change the pawn structure, by leaving the defending side with some type of weakness (e.g. an isolated pawn, or a backward pawn). Let's show the attack in action. } 1. { -KEY- } b4 { White begins advancing their queenside pawn minority. The purpose of this move is to prepare the eventual b4-b5 pawn break. Once the pawn break is on the board, Black will be left with an unpleasant choice. } Kg7 { We'll shuffle Black's king around to illustrate the theme. Most opponents are not going to be quite so obliging of course! As in the case of the pawn breaks introduction, this is a 'barebones structure'. The minority attack can vary greatly in strength depending on the piece arrangement for both sides. } 2. a4 { White is now ready to break with b4-b5. In a real game, you may not actually need the support of your a-pawn, since for example you might have a rook on b1. } Kg8 3. b5 { The pawn break is on the board! Black will have two options most of the time here (ignore, or capture). Occasionally, Black will have a third possibility (to advance the pawn) though this is unlikely given that White possesses the semi-open c-file so the c5 square tends to be better controlled by the attacker. } Kg7 { Most of the time the defending side's best bet will be to ignore the pawn break. This is however not without its own set of problems. } ( 3... cxb5 { This is usually undesirable for the defending side, since it leaves an isolated pawn on d5. } 4. axb5 { After the exchange on b5, White no longer possesses the semi-open c-file but instead has a semi-open a-file. Oftentimes, White will have a rook on a1 and is ready to exploit this. It's also really hard for Black to advance their queenside pawns because if they ever play ...a6 then after bxa6 bxa6 Black will be left with a second isolated pawn. } ) 
 ( 3... c5 $6 { Here of course this move would lose a pawn, but even if we assume that after dxc5 Black had a piece that was ready to recapture on c5, the problem is that Black is still left with a very weak isolated pawn on d5. Additionally, after the exchange of the d4 and c5 pawns, there is a newly created semi-open d-file that White can use to add more pressure against Black's IQP. } ) 
4. bxc6 { Usually White will wish to capture here. } bxc6 { Part of the power of pawn breaks is that usually the side that is breaking can decide when they would like to exchange pawns. In the minority attack, White will pick and choose when to open up the b-file. A second structural problem for Black is that their c6 pawn has now lost the defense of the base pawn on b7. The result is that Black's c-pawn is now a backward pawn. Not only will Black's pieces face the unpleasant task of constantly safeguarding this pawn, but additionallly White now enjoys a powerful outpost on the c5 square, which in practice is often occupied by a knight or rook. White has also incurred some structural damage as a result of this pawn break with an isolated pawn on a4. What matters, however, is the relative damage sustained by both sides. Before the minority attack both sides had two pawn islands. This is still the case for White, while Black now has three. Hopefully this introduction has helped you to understand the general logic behind this common positional strategy. Let's turn to some real life examples now. } 
