 { In this first example, taken from an old game played in 1948 (Lilienthal,A - Lundin,E) White launched a Minority Attack, and Black did not react in a convincing way. } 20. { -KEY- } b5 $1 axb5 21. axb5 cxb5 $2 { Black offers no resistance, and immediately gives White what he wants. Now Black is left with two isolated pawns on b7 and d5. } ( 21... Rc7 $5 { A move such as this one would have offered much greater resistance. } 22. bxc6 bxc6 { White stands better since they can pressure Black's c-pawn for a long time, but at least Black has kept their structural damage to a minimum. } ( 22... Rxc6 { Capturing with the rook would have led to the same structure as in the game, but at least here Black's piece activity is a little better. } 23. Qb3 $14 { White enjoys a long-term edge due to the better structure. } ) 
23. Na4 $14 { Likely White should continue by chasing after the c5 outpost. Black can take the game in a few directions. Moves like } Rca7 { or } Bh5 { seem logical to me, following the guiding principle that when you have the worse structure it's helpful to look for active piece play to generate counter-chances. } ) 
22. Nxb5 { The Minority Attack has succeeded. Black is left with two isolated pawns on b7 and d5. } Rc8 23. Qa2 $16 ( { White enjoyed a pleasant positional edge and went on to win the game, although } 23. Qb3 { would have been even more logical, as from there the White queen would apply pressure onto both of Black's isolated pawns on d5 and b7. } ) 

