 { This example is taken from the game Astaneh Lopez,A - Goodger,M (2008) . Here the Minority Attack is particularly effective since it not only pressures Black's queenside pawn formation, but it also allows White to activate the dark-squared bishop. } 17. { -KEY- } b5 $1 { Both the b5 pawn and the a3 pawns are left undefended, but Black cannot capture them in good circumstances. } Nf6 { Ignoring the pawn may have been Black's best bet. } ( 17... Bxa3 $2 { This was my opponent's choice in the game. However, it fails tactically. } 18. bxc6 { Black lands in immediate trouble, as it is not possible to capture back on c6 without huge material losses. } bxc6 ( 18... Nf6 { was played in the game, but after } 19. Rfb1 Bd6 20. Rxa4 Rxa4 21. Qxa4 bxc6 22. Qxc6 $18 { White is up a pawn and with a better structure. } ) 
19. Rxa3 $1 { This temporary exchange sacrifice wins material by force. } Qxa3 20. Bb4 Qb3 21. Qxc6 $18 { Black's position collapses completely, as they are unable to fend off the multiple threats. } ) 
 ( 17... cxb5 $2 { This leaves Black's d5 pawn undefended. } 18. Bxd5 Kh8 19. Rfb1 $18 { The d5 pawn is much more valuable than White's b-pawn. Additionally, Black has no good way of defending their weak pawn on b5. } ) 
18. Rfb1 $1 ( 18. bxc6 $6 bxc6 19. Bb4 $16 { White stands much better here, as they can target Black's backward c6 pawn as well as aim to drive their knight to the c5 outpost in the future. However, here Black has } Ba6 $1 { when at least Black manages to trade off White's strong d3 knight. } 20. Rfb1 Bxd3 21. Qxd3 $16 { White is still much better, but from a practical standpoint it's a relief for Black that they don't have to worry about White's knight jumping onto either e5 or c5 in the near future. In comparison 18.Rfb1 is a bit more suffocating for Black. } ) 
Bd7 19. bxc6 bxc6 20. Bb4 $1 { Trading off dark-squared bishops allows White to secure a superior minor piece dynamic, a topic previously discussed in the 'Piece Play' chapter. } Ra7 21. Bxd6 Qxd6 22. Rb4 $18 { White has achieved a lot of positional goals with the Minority Attack. They have a better structure, superior minor pieces, pressure along the b- and c-files, and an outpost on c5. So far we've seen the damage a Minority Attack can do to an unprepared opponent. Let's close out the section with a couple of examples of how you can defend against this positional attack. } 
