 { In this example we'll cover a couple of typical methods to defend against the Minority Attack. Since a minority attack is a pawn break, we can use the same defensive techniques previously learned. These were: stopping the advance, positioning the pieces, and counter-breaking. } 1. { -KEY- } Nxc6 $5 ( 1. Nd3 $1 { One approach is to position your pieces ideally. In this case, the knight covers b4 as well as eyes up the outpost on c5. } a5 2. a3 { White continues to stop the attack. } Qd6 { Black insists on making the break even against ideal positioning. Here White can keep improving the positioning of their pieces with a move like } 3. Rac1 { . However, they can also switch to a more direct approach: } 3. b4 $11 { Stopping the pawn break, at the cost of accepting a backward pawn on c3. The other major upshot for White is that they now have a power outpost on c5, making the game hang in the balance. In the 1.Nd3 variation we explored the defensive methods of positioning the pieces, and of stopping the advance directly. With the move 1.Nxc6, White goes for the third option of counter-breaking. } ) 
Rxc6 2. a4 $1 { White is first to get in the pawn break, latching on to Black's advanced b-pawn. } ( 2. a3 $6 a5 { would be a lot less fun in practice. Black will play ...Qd6 next and prepare to inflict structural damage upon White with ...b4 soon. } ) 
a6 3. axb5 axb5 { The a-file opens up. } 4. b4 $5 { Once again White goes for the backward pawn setup. This time, however, you may be asking yourself why it would be appealing considering that White no longer has a knight to place on the c5 power outpost. The answer to this is that it's not quite as appealing as before. This time it's more a defensive configuration aimed at equality. White accepts one weakness on c3, but is banking on two things to hold the balance: 1. Black's pawn on b5 is fixed and can be targeted in the future. 2. White only has one weakness, and it can be easily defended by all the heavy pieces, making it difficult for Black to make progress. } Qc7 5. Ra3 Rc8 6. Re3 $11 { White has no problems due to the active defensive configuration. In practice, Black may even need to be the more cautious one as White has some offensive possibilities on the kingside. Let's take a look at one final example of defense to close out the section. } 
