 { In this position Magnus Carlsen had the White pieces against GM Aman Hambleton. Black is well-configured to play for the Minority Attack, so White reroutes one of his pieces to the ideal square. Can you find the move Magnus played? } 1. { -KEY- } Nc1 $1 { Correct! The knight is headed for the d3 square as in the previous example. } Nc4 2. Qe2 a5 { Black is ready to break, but White is just in time to stop it. } 3. Nd3 $1 { Now Black is unable to get the break in. } Bd6 { In the game Black pivoted and offered a bishop trade, indicating that he had given up on his plan to try to play the ...b4 break. } ( 3... b4 $2 { The sacrificial pawn break is not a good idea. } 4. axb4 axb4 5. Nxb4 { Capturing with the knight forces Black to part ways with their strong bishop if they wish to damage White's structure. } Bxb4 6. cxb4 $16 { White will play b2-b3 next, and it's not at all clear how Black can recover their pawn deficit. } ) 
4. Bxd6 $14 { With an approximately equal position, though if anyone is slightly better it's White since they have a semi-open e-file in their favour that gives them some chances to make progress on the kingside, while Black's queenside progress has ground to a halt. This brings us to the end of the section, where we've examined various methods for targeting the opponent's structure with your pawns. In the next section, we'll look at how you can use your pawns to target the enemy's pieces, specifically bishops and knights. } 
